=head1 NAME

afsio_lsmount, afsio_fidlsmount - Reports the volume for which a directory is
the mount point

=head1 SYNOPSIS

=for html
<div class="synopsis">

B<afsio lsmount> S<<< B<-dir> <I<AFS-dirname>>+ >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-verbose>] [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>]
    S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

B<afsio fidlsmount> S<<< B<-fid> <I<volume.vnode.uniquifier>>+ >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-verbose>] [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>]
    S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

=for html
</div>

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The B<afsio lsmount> command reports the volume for which each specified
directory is a mount point, or indicates with an error message that a
directory is not a mount point.

To create a mount point, use the B<afsio mkmount> command. To remove one, use
the B<afsio rmmount> command.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item B<-dir> <I<AFS-dirname>>+

Specifies each of the directories that serve as a mount point for a volume. A
path not beginning with F</afs> is interpreted relative to the cell's
F<root.cell>. A path beginning with F</> but not F</afs> is invalid.

=item B<-fid> <I<volume.vnode.uniquifier>>+

Specifies each directory that serve as a mount point for a volume as a FID
triple (e.g., C<536870915.1.1>). This option is used by B<afsio fidlsmount>.

=item B<-cell> <I<cell name>>

Specifies the AFS cell to operate in.

=item B<-realm> <I<realm name>>

Specifies the Kerberos realm to use for authentication.

=item B<-verbose>

Has no effect for B<afsio lsmount> or B<afsio fidlsmount>.

=item B<-clear>

Uses unencrypted (cleartext) connections to file servers.

=item B<-crypt>

Uses encrypted connections to file servers.

=item B<-as-user> <I<username>>

Uses localauth credentials to pretend to be the given user, allowing
authenticated RXAFS operations without a running kernel client.

=item B<-help>

Prints the online help for this command. All other valid options are
ignored.

=back

=head1 OUTPUT

If the specified directory is a mount point, the output is of the
following form:

   '<directory>' is a mount point for volume '<volume name>'

where

=over 4

=item *

A number sign (C<#>) precedes the <volume name> string for a regular mount
point.

=item *

A percent sign (C<%>) precedes the <volume name> string for a read/write
mount point.

=item *

A cell name and colon (C<:>) follow the number or percent sign and precede
the <volume name> string for a cellular mount point.

=back

If the directory is not a mount point or is not in AFS, the output reads:

   afsio: Invalid argument (is not a valid mount point: '<directory>')

=head1 EXAMPLES

The following example shows the mount point for the home directory of user
C<smith>:

   % afsio lsmount /afs/example.com/usr/smith
   '/afs/example.com/usr/smith' is a mount point for volume '#user.smith'

The following example shows both the regular and read/write mount points
for the Example Corporation cell's C<root.cell> volume.

   % afsio lsmount /afs/example.com
   '/afs/example.com' is a mount point for volume '#root.cell'

   % afsio lsmount /afs/.example.com
   '/afs/.example.com' is a mount point for volume '%root.cell'

The following example shows a cellular mount point: the Example Organization
cell's C<root.cell> volume as mounted in the Example Corporation cell's tree.

   % afsio lsmount /afs/example.org
   '/afs/example.org' is a mount point for volume '#example.org:root.cell'

=head1 PRIVILEGE REQUIRED

The issuer must have the C<l> (lookup) permission on the ACL of the root
directory of the volume that houses the file or directory named by the
B<-dir> argument, and on the ACL of each directory that precedes it in the
pathname.

